Install
=======

See REQUIREMENTS for the hardware/software each tier needs and STATUS for
which badges this artifact claims and why. This file is the step-by-step
walkthrough; every step ends with the output that confirms it worked.

0. Clone
--------
    git clone https://github.com/amanyagami/viyog.git
    cd viyog

1. Environment
--------------
    uv sync

Expected: creates `.venv/` and installs torch, numpy (and pytest/ruff for
dev). No errors.

Tier T0 -- install & sanity check (CPU, ~2 minutes)
-------------------------------------------------------
    uv run pytest tests/ -q

Expected: `12 passed`.

    uv run python examples/quickstart.py --smoke

Expected output ends with three AUROC lines, e.g.:

    T2  ID  vs ADV : AUROC=1.000   (adversarial detection)
    T3  OOD vs ADV : AUROC=1.000   (OOD-vs-ADV separation)
    T1  ID  vs OOD : AUROC=1.000   (weak; use a logit score)

(`--smoke` uses synthetic data as a pure code sanity-check -- these AUROCs
are not the paper's numbers. Tier T1 below reproduces the real ones.)

This confirms the installed `viyog` package -- the artifact itself -- runs
end-to-end and its statistic behaves as documented (higher V(x) for ADV).

Tier T1 -- core recompute (GPU, the Reproducible-badge claim)
------------------------------------------------------------------
    uv sync --group experiments

Expected: adds torchattacks, timm, pytorch-ood, h5py, pandas, fvcore, and the
rest of the experiments/ pipeline's dependencies to `.venv/`.

    uv run python experiments/01_download.py --core-only

Expected: fetches 6 checkpoints (~250 MB total) from
`huggingface.co/amanyagami/viyog-weights` and ends with:

    All required (core) weights present.

    export CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0   # whichever GPU you want to use
    uv run python experiments/03_gen_adversarial.py \
        --models convnextv2_base densenet121 mobilenetv3_l resnet50 swin_tiny vit_base \
        --attacks fgsm bim pgd apgd_ce

Expected: writes `data/adversarial/<model>_<attack>.h5` for all 24
model x attack combinations; ends with a per-file size listing and no errors.
CIFAR-100 downloads automatically on first run if not already present.

    uv run python experiments/06b_extract_full.py \
        --models convnextv2_base densenet121 mobilenetv3_l resnet50 swin_tiny vit_base \
        --attacks fgsm bim pgd apgd_ce

Expected: writes `results/features/featfull_<model>_<split>.h5` for the ID,
10 OOD, and 4 ADV splits of each of the 6 models. The 10 OOD benchmark
datasets auto-download via torchvision on first use (see REQUIREMENTS for
disk/network notes -- this step's wall-clock is dominated by those
downloads, not compute).

    uv run python experiments/09_signatures_full.py \
        --models convnextv2_base densenet121 mobilenetv3_l resnet50 swin_tiny vit_base

Expected: prints the best signature per task per model and writes
`results/analysis/signature_auroc_full_<model>.csv`.

    uv run python experiments/full_eval.py --dataset cifar100

Expected: prints per-task tables (T1/T2/T3) ranking every detector, and
writes `results/analysis/full_eval_cifar100_summary.csv`. Check the
`ViyogD_tv_dorm` row in the "T2: ID-vs-ADV" table against README's
"Expected headline numbers" table:

    T2 mean_dl ~ 0.98   T3 mean_dl ~ 0.84   (this 6-model core tier, run with the
    four attacks used in the steps above -- NOT the paper's 0.966/0.824 headline,
    which averages the full 20-arch panel; see README's "Reproducing the paper"
    section for why these differ and why that's expected)

A clean-clone run of exactly these steps on a single H200 (2026-07-29) measured
0.9804 / 0.8441 in 4 h 7 m. Small deviations from seed/hardware nondeterminism
are expected; large deviations -- off by more than a few hundredths of AUROC --
indicate a setup problem worth reporting.

Note if you compare against the committed CSVs in `results/analysis/`: those
were generated over the full SIX-attack suite (the four above plus `deepfool`
and `cw`) and read 0.940 / 0.777. DeepFool and CW are minimal-perturbation
attacks and are much harder to detect (T2 ~0.70-0.78 on their own), so
including them lowers the mean. Both numbers are correct for their respective
attack sets -- README's "Reproducing the paper" section has the per-attack
breakdown.

    uv run python experiments/exp_master_table.py --dataset cifar100

Expected: prints a ranked detector table (reads the full_eval_cifar100_summary.csv
just written above) and writes `results/analysis/master_comparison_cifar100.csv`
with the same T2/T3 numbers, alongside the logit/distance baseline comparison.

Optional -- regenerate the paper's figures/tables from these results:

    uv run python experiments/plot_t3_breakdown.py --out /tmp/fig_t3.pdf
    # (see the Makefile `figures` target for the other figure scripts)

Tier T2 -- extended (optional)
-------------------------------
Repeat the T1 commands above without `--core-only` on 01_download.py and
without `--models`/`--attacks` restrictions (defaults = all 20 architectures,
all 6 attacks) on the later steps. Expect proportionally longer runtime and
more disk.

Demo (optional, no GPU/weights needed)
-----------------------------------------
    make demo-data && make demo

Expected: rebuilds `demo/data/*.csv` from `results/analysis/` and opens the
interactive Viyog-vs-pytorch-ood leaderboard at http://localhost:7860.
